#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128841 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129264 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7a75441-d233-5a64-8d87-b3138c2d31be" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "0e34fc48-0bfc-57b3-82fd-e5ed205995a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11372" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=4.$\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R (y-z)\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin. \nAs a result, in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq \\theta \\lt 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=(y-z)\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =(\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & ={\\rho }^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(y-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}{\\rho }^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128842 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129265 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b1d5b6a-3e7b-567d-a61f-d18f55924912" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Shifted Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "c56b3218-2c96-59fe-a5e3-af6f01164af4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11373" :question/problem "![](images/95/95bb2c41fc54db270b8f6c884767868a8c691372a5d0910109f67582ef361ac6.png)\n\n \n\nThe region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=10x$ for $y \\leq 0$ and $z \\leq 0,$ shown above.\nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_R \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}} \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-quarter of the sphere of radius $5$ centered at $(5,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^2+y^2+z^2=10x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =10x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =10\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi.$\n\n![](images/53/53c68b2181ffbc7bd4a47206f67ee3da2af19d5f80683fd5ecb367c7d57bd47a.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 10\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\:\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi , \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{\\rho }\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128843 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ea201b-cf90-54cf-bd20-d7e990345710" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/id #uuid "85182b2a-10d4-5663-831b-41623c395cce" :question/math-academy-id "e-11374" :question/problem "![](images/48/48a7c7ea2614c0b46df169cf353183f5b862f5feabab97a050107fe227f1521a.png)\n\n \n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R 2z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere $x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 9$ for $y \\leq 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\n\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \n\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $3$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/cd/cd2f48915774902314c38f2b5e40baa646761ae14b560eab401d8841f61f5612.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 3, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z) = 2z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2 + z^2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot \\rho  \\\\  & =2{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}2{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }d\\theta \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\rho }^{4}\\,d\\rho  \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2ϕ]}_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi }\\cdot [\\theta ]_{\\theta =\\pi }^{\\theta =2\\pi }\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{5}{\\rho }^{5}]}_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(1-1)\\cdot (2\\pi -\\pi )\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(243-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\pi \\cdot \\frac{243}{5} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d85b11c-498d-53b7-98f8-b742b46e8dc2" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Part-Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "2adb62b7-c889-5c4c-be0f-8f26a240475c" :question/math-academy-id "e-12409" :question/problem "The region $R$ is enclosed inside the sphere $x^2+y^2+z^2=4$ for $y \\leq 0.$ \nExpress the following triple integral in spherical polar coordinates. \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\iiint\\limits_R \\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2+z^2} \\:\\textrm{d}V\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\, \\mathrm{d}V = \n\\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\, f(\\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\rho\\cos\\phi) \\:  \\rho^2\\sin\\phi \\: \\mathrm{d}\\rho \\, \\mathrm{d}\\theta \\, \\mathrm{d}\\phi.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad \nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $2$ centered at the origin.\n\n![](images/46/4689af5de2bb93606060ff95ee2f2af1d34206c3ab89be8f854108c855a00aa3.png)\n\n  \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (\\rho, \\theta, \\phi) \\, : \\, 0 \\leq \\rho \\leq 2, \\: \\pi \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi, \\: 0 \\leq \\phi \\leq \\pi \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\dfrac{1}{x^2+y^2+z^2}{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129266 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00ea201b-cf90-54cf-bd20-d7e990345710" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral by Converting to Spherical Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6a5a00d-d3a2-592c-881e-77bbd7ca4767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371963} {:answer/id #uuid "6ad28b97-cf66-543e-bc10-3ba737571a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371964} {:answer/id #uuid "a500a19d-b4be-5b7f-9500-d5b8c2591ac4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371965} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9ed56b-8382-5e99-8dba-fe3dba49dc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371966} {:answer/id #uuid "a90c56fb-b2ba-5c26-85e9-9ab62878d8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6ad28b97-cf66-543e-bc10-3ba737571a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371964} :answer-field/id #uuid "518e9126-5f8e-5218-940a-7438176b2bce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6e23e7ac-d75a-538e-9787-5d9be89e54a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161597" :question/problem "![](images/ce/ce36be89987c16762ad2b2e21d38cf78ec565f0aada219c982da6ea5eb958c1c.png)\n\nEvaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}V\n$$\n\nover the region $R$ enclosed inside the sphere ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2x$ for $z\\ge 0,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,dV={∭}_{Δ}\\,f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\,{\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ.\n$$\n\nIf $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $R$ represents one-half of the sphere of radius $1$ centered at $(1,0,0).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2x$ in spherical polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2} & =2x \\\\ {\\rho }^{2} & =2\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ \\\\ \\rho (\\rho -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $\\rho $ cannot be identical to zero on the entire sphere, we must have $\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ.$\n\n![](images/31/31a18ebb9608ff60a4a5ecbcfa8defcb6a64337b126cb5859cb042f1019b1194.png)\n\n \n\nAs a result, we obtain that in spherical polar coordinates, our region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)\\,:\\,0\\le \\rho \\le 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le ϕ\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the spherical polar coordinates into the function $f(x,y,z)=\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z) & =f(\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{\\sqrt{({\\rho }^{2})^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\frac{3z}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}V & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ}{{\\rho }^{2}}\\cdot {\\rho }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\,d\\rho \\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ[\\rho ]_{\\rho =0}^{\\rho =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ-0)\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,d\\theta \\,dϕ \\\\  & =2\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ\\,dϕ\\cdot {\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,d\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot [-{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot [{sin}^{3}⁡ϕ]_{ϕ=0}^{ϕ=\\pi /2}\\cdot [\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]_{\\theta =-\\pi /2}^{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d85b11c-498d-53b7-98f8-b742b46e8dc2" :knowledge-point/title "Rewriting a Triple Integral Defined Over a Part-Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9bf1487b-2b67-56f7-89d5-e814082fa376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}ρ 2 sin ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126644} {:answer/id #uuid "c27c4657-91d3-5e16-9277-03f96c52d877" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}ρ 3 cos 2 ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126645} {:answer/id #uuid "db03b97f-5a67-5631-992e-0192bc55fe65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π / 2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3}ρ 2 cos ⁡ ϕ sin ⁡ ϕdρdθdϕ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186126646} {:answer/id #uuid "32952c1b-b532-5ae9-b6a8-b498784a